let __v_70 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"tins\",{\"$tuple\":[\"tin\",\"tins\",\"tin can\",\"tin cans\",\"cans\",\"drink can\",\"drink cans\",\"food tin\",\"food tins\",\"baked bean tin\",\"soup tin\",\"pet food tin\",\"aluminium\"]},\"Tins and cans go in the kerbside recycling: rinse them, leave them whole and push the lid inside, and do not flatten them - the plant sorts steel from aluminium, and a flattened tin can be read as the wrong metal. An aerosol is the one can that goes back to a shop or the tip if it still has something in it.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"foil\",{\"$tuple\":[\"foil\",\"tin foil\",\"kitchen foil\",\"foil tray\",\"foil trays\",\"foil container\",\"bacofoil\"]},\"Clean foil goes in the kerbside recycling, scrunched into a ball about the size of a fist so the machine can pick it out - loose foil and a foil tray with food stuck to it do not get sorted, and a greasy one goes in the general waste.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"jars and bottles\",{\"$tuple\":[\"jar\",\"jars\",\"glass jar\",\"glass jars\",\"glass bottle\",\"glass bottles\",\"wine bottle\",\"beer bottle\",\"jam jar\",\"sauce jar\"]},\"Glass jars and bottles go in the kerbside recycling: empty and rinsed, lids off, and the lids go in with the metal if they are metal. Drinking glasses, Pyrex and ovenware are a different glass and go in the general waste, because they melt at the wrong temperature.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"paper and card\",{\"$tuple\":[\"paper\",\"newspaper\",\"newspapers\",\"card\",\"cardboard\",\"carton\",\"cartons\",\"junk mail\",\"envelope\",\"envelopes\",\"magazine\",\"magazines\",\"cereal box\",\"cereal boxes\"]},\"Paper and card go in the kerbside recycling, flattened and dry: envelopes with a window, cereal boxes and junk mail all go in. A food carton or a tetra pak is only collected where the council says so, and a greasy pizza box goes in the general waste apart from its clean lid.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"plastic bottles and tubs\",{\"$tuple\":[\"plastic bottle\",\"plastic bottles\",\"bottle\",\"bottles\",\"tub\",\"tubs\",\"yoghurt pot\",\"yoghurt pots\",\"margarine tub\",\"plastic tub\",\"plastic tubs\",\"milk bottle\",\"shampoo bottle\"]},\"Plastic bottles and tubs go in the kerbside recycling: rinse them, squash the bottles, and leave the lids on or off as your council says, because both are collected in most places now. A black plastic tray is the one that is often not sorted, and your council's A to Z page will say.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"soft plastic\",{\"$tuple\":[\"crisp packet\",\"crisp packets\",\"carrier bag\",\"carrier bags\",\"plastic bag\",\"plastic bags\",\"bread bag\",\"film lid\",\"cling film\",\"bubble wrap\",\"soft plastic\",\"padded envelope\"]},\"Soft plastic - carrier bags, crisp packets, bread bags, film lids, bubble wrap - is not collected at the kerbside in most places. The big supermarkets take it in a bin by the door, and the collected plastic is turned into bin bags and pellets. A padded envelope is the same, and it is worth keeping a bag by the door for it.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"polystyrene\",{\"$tuple\":[\"polystyrene\",\"styrofoam\",\"packing peanuts\",\"meat tray\",\"meat trays\",\"takeaway box\",\"takeaway boxes\"]},\"Polystyrene goes in the general waste, not the recycling: expanded foam is mostly air, and no kerbside service takes it. A clean meat tray is the exception in some areas because some trays are the recyclable kind - squeeze it, and if it springs back it is foam and goes in the bin.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"nappies\",{\"$tuple\":[\"nappy\",\"nappies\",\"diaper\",\"diapers\",\"baby wipes\",\"wet wipes\",\"sanitary\",\"sanitary pad\",\"sanitary pads\",\"tampon\",\"tampons\",\"incontinence\"]},\"Nappies, wipes, pads and tampons all go in the general waste - nothing absorbent is recycled, and a wet wipe is not flushable however the packet reads, because it does not break down and it is most of what blocks a sewer. Bag it and bin it; a nappy bin with a lid keeps the room livable.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"clothes and textiles\",{\"$tuple\":[\"clothes\",\"clothing\",\"old clothes\",\"textiles\",\"textile\",\"shoes\",\"old shoes\",\"curtains\",\"bedding\",\"duvet\",\"duvets\",\"towels\",\"rags\"]},\"Clothes, shoes, bedding and curtains do not go in the kerbside bin: the charity bank or the textile bank at the supermarket car park takes them, even worn ones, and they are sorted into resale, rags and fibre. A duvet and a pillow are the awkward ones - most banks will not take them, so ring the council for a bulky collection or take them to the tip's textile container.\"]}]}")?;
